{"objective":"Put a genuinely usable communication tool in the team's hands in one increment — text, presence and asynchronous voice notes, on builds that work on every device in the fleet — while proving the protocol, the reconnect behaviour and the audio toolchain on real hardware before any real-time voice work is attempted.\n\nThe MVP is deliberately shaped so that the two hardest problems in the original feature set, real-time audio latency and a direct peer-to-peer data plane, are outside it. What remains still delivers all five use cases from the vision except collaborative debugging.","scope":"## Coordinator\n\n- A tsnet node joining the tailnet directly from its container; no host networking, no Tailscale sidecar.\n- WebSocket control plane carrying protobuf envelopes.\n- Authentication by WhoIs lookup on the connection's remote address. No credentials stored anywhere.\n- Presence registry with online state derived from an observed heartbeat and a liveness TTL.\n- Per-recipient offline queue with monotonic positions, acknowledgement, retention TTL and a size cap that refuses rather than fails silently.\n- Structured JSON logs, a metrics endpoint, a health endpoint — all bound to the tailnet interface only.\n\n## Client\n\n- Single binary; the default build is pure Go and fully static.\n- Terminal UI: message pane, device list showing presence and custom status, input line.\n- Direct messages and broadcast.\n- Local history persisting both the sender's send time and the coordinator's receive time.\n- ULID per message, with duplicate suppression at the receiver.\n- Offline outbox: messages composed while disconnected are sent on reconnect.\n- Reconnect with exponentially backed-off, jittered retries; heartbeat; session resumption by acknowledged position rather than full handshake.\n- A local control socket, so the running client is scriptable from the shell.\n\n## Voice note — behind the `voice` build tag\n\n- Toggle capture (tap to start, tap to send), because a terminal cannot report key release portably.\n- Opus encode, delivery through the coordinator as an attachment, playback on receipt.\n- Legible degradation: a build without audio support receives the voice note and tells the user it cannot be played locally.\n\n## Encryption\n\n- Sealed-box encryption of payloads that rest in the coordinator's queue.\n- X25519 identity keypair generated on first run, stored with restrictive permissions.\n- Public-key distribution through the coordinator; trust on first use; a readable fingerprint; a loud, confirmation-requiring warning when a known peer's key changes.\n\n## Delivery\n\n- Default build released for linux/amd64, linux/arm64, linux/arm, darwin/arm64, darwin/amd64, windows/amd64.\n- Audio build released for linux/amd64, linux/arm64 and darwin/arm64, cross-compiled using zig cc.\n- Coordinator published as a container image.\n\n## Testing\n\n- A deterministic network harness with injectable packet loss, latency and partition, driven by a fake clock, so reconnect and queue-drain behaviour is tested reproducibly rather than by hand.\n- A null audio backend so continuous integration needs no sound hardware.\n- Golden encode/decode tests for the protobuf schema.\n- The cross-compilation matrix exercised in continuous integration.\n\n## Documentation\n\n- A quickstart reaching a working session in no more than three commands, written for a non-technical reader, and pointing explicitly to Tailscale SSH for remote shell access.\n- `walkie help` covering every command.\n- An operations runbook for the coordinator, including the tailnet ACL expectations that walkie inherits and cannot compensate for.\n\n## Sequencing constraint\n\nThe audio-toolchain spike runs **first and blocks** the voice-note work. If it fails on real ARM hardware, the voice note leaves this scope and the MVP becomes text and presence only — a consequence defined in advance so the decision does not have to be improvised under pressure.","outOfScope":"Everything below is deliberately excluded from this increment. Each item is either deferred to a named later phase in the roadmap, or declined outright.\n\n**Deferred to phase 2** — real-time one-to-one voice calls and the sub-100 ms latency target, which does **not** apply to anything in this scope; the direct peer-to-peer data plane; the relay-fallback implementation; direct file transfer with resume; the direct-versus-relay and latency indicators in the interface.\n\n**Deferred to phase 3** — group chat and rooms with persistence, invite and removal; read-only PTY streaming for collaborative debugging.\n\n**Deferred to phase 4** — slash commands and aliases; webhooks and external service integration; the subprocess plugin protocol; the auto-update mechanism.\n\n**Deferred without a phase** — key rotation and multi-device identity; encrypted local message history; rich text formatting, emoji handling and attachment preview beyond voice notes. These are recorded gaps rather than scheduled work, and the security model names the first two explicitly.\n\n**Declined outright** — remote command execution, which is delegated to Tailscale SSH by decision, not deferred. Recorded in adr:005-no-remote-command-execution so that its absence reads as a decision rather than an omission."}